Types of Prohibited Acts and Criteria Therefor (Re: Article 15(1))
■ Enforcement Decree of Internet Multimedia Broadcast Services Act [Appendix 3]

Types of Prohibited Acts and Criteria Therefor (Re: Article 15(1))

1. Refusal to provide services

The prohibited act under Article 17(1)1 of the Act shall be any of the following:

(a) Refusing a particular user's request to use Internet multimedia broadcast services within the relevant service area of an Internet multimedia broadcast service provider without a justifiable ground, such as a shortage of facilities;

(b) Refusing to supply a particular service to a particular user without a justifiable ground;

(c) Unilaterally suspending the provision of Internet multimedia broadcast services or terminating a service agreement without a justifiable ground, even though no ground prescribed in the terms and conditions of use, such as overdue charges, exists;

(d) Refusing, without a justifiable ground, to provide Internet multimedia broadcast services alone when selling those services bundled with other telecommunications services or similar services.

2. Provision of services or billing of service charges contrary to the terms and conditions of use

The prohibited act under Article 17(1)2 of the Act shall be any of the following:

(a) Unfairly providing services on terms different from the terms and conditions of use, thereby substantially infringing the interests of the relevant user;

(b) Unfairly billing charges on terms different from the terms and conditions of use, thereby substantially infringing the interests of the relevant user;

(c) Providing services without confirming the user's intention to use them, thereby substantially infringing the user's interests.

3. Improper misuse of user information

The prohibited act under Article 17(1)3 of the Act shall be any of the following:

(a) Improperly disclosing user information learned in the course of providing Internet multimedia broadcast services, or providing it to a third party;

(b) Improperly using user information learned in the course of providing Internet multimedia broadcast services for the service provider's own business activities.

4. Unfair discrimination against users

The prohibited act under Article 17(1)4 of the Act shall be any of the following:

(a) Unfairly discriminating against a user by providing or proposing service charges, conditions of use, equipment for use, or similar matters relating to Internet multimedia broadcast services on terms substantially more favorable or unfavorable than those offered to other users;

(b) Providing Internet multimedia broadcast services under a service agreement with charges or conditions of use substantially less favorable than those applicable to other users, by deliberately providing discriminatory service information to a particular user, omitting the fact that a particular service is offered for sale, or engaging in similar conduct;

(c) When selling Internet multimedia broadcast services bundled with other telecommunications or broadcasting services or similar services, providing Internet multimedia broadcast services at favorable charges or on favorable conditions of use by continuously discriminating in a substantially unfair manner compared with users who use Internet multimedia broadcast services alone.

5. Abuse of a superior position against Internet multimedia broadcast content providers

The prohibited act under Article 17(1)5 of the Act shall be any of the following:

(a) Using a superior position to unfairly force an Internet multimedia broadcast content provider to enter into a substantially unfavorable contract, such as by limiting the liability for damages of the Internet multimedia broadcast service provider or limiting the content provider's right to terminate the contract;

(b) Using a superior position to unfairly cause an Internet multimedia broadcast content provider to refuse to conclude a content supply agreement with another Internet multimedia broadcast service provider, or to cause or compel it to conclude a contract on unfairly discriminatory terms;

(c) Using a superior position to unfairly demand from an Internet multimedia broadcast content provider a substantially unfavorable distribution of revenue or economic benefits excessive in comparison with normal practice, or to compel it to bear substantial economic losses.

6. Interference with another broadcasting business operator's business

The prohibited act under Article 17(1)6 of the Act shall be any of the following:

(a) Unfairly interfering with the provision of services, such as by damaging the facilities or equipment of another Internet multimedia broadcast service provider, for the purpose of obstructing the use of that provider's services;

(b) Unfairly obstructing the conclusion of a service agreement between another Internet multimedia broadcast service provider and a user, through delays in subscriber switching, improper inducements, excessive benefits, or similar means, for the purpose of interfering with that provider's business;

(c) Unfairly obstructing the conclusion of a service agreement by providing false information about charges, conditions of use, or service quality in relation to another Internet multimedia broadcast service provider's services or the provider's own Internet multimedia broadcast services;

(d) Unfairly entering into an exclusive service agreement with a user that excludes the provision of Internet multimedia broadcast services by another Internet multimedia broadcast service provider.

7. Refusal, suspension, or restriction of the use of or access to essential telecommunications facilities

The prohibited act under Article 17(1)7 of the Act shall be any of the following:

(a) Refusing to consult or conclude a contract, or failing to perform a concluded contract or similar arrangement, without a justifiable ground, when another Internet multimedia broadcast service provider requests the use of or access to telecommunications facilities essential for providing its services;

(b) Blocking or restricting, without a justifiable ground, telecommunications facilities that another Internet multimedia broadcast service provider is lawfully using under a contract or similar arrangement;

(c) Restricting the use of or access to telecommunications facilities by offering, without a justifiable ground, prices or conditions of use that discriminate in comparison with those applicable to another Internet multimedia broadcast service provider using the facilities.
